INT. TRELAWNEY HOUSE - FRONT HALL - LATE AFTERNOON

A long hallway, floored in black-and-white tile, lined with portraits of unsmiling ancestors. A grandfather clock. Dust in the window light. A telephone table with an old answering machine blinking red.

The machine clicks. Whirs.

AGNES (V.O.)
(recorded, bright)
You've reached the Trelawney residence. We can't come to the phone right now. Leave a message and we'll ring you back.

BEEP.

AGNES (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded, older, hoarse)
Hello? It's me. It's Agnes. I know you're there. Pick up.

Silence. The machine clicks off.

The front door opens. AGNES TRELAWNEY, 70s, in a long wool coat, rain on her shoulders, steps in. A battered leather suitcase in one hand, a key on a lanyard around her neck. She shuts the door slowly and stands listening to the house.

AGNES
Hello?

The clock ticks. She sets down the suitcase and crosses to the telephone table.

AGNES (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Four messages. Who calls a house that has been empty for a year?

She presses the button.

AGNES (V.O.)
(recorded, young)
Mother, it's me. Please call me back.

She stops the machine. Presses it again.

AGNES (V.O.)
(recorded, young)
Mother, it's me.

She stops it. Her hand trembles on the casing.

AGNES
That was my voice.

She lifts the receiver, listens to the dial tone, and sets it back. In the mirror above the table, the hall behind her is empty.

AGNES (CONT'D)
(softly)
It's me, mother. I'm home.

Somewhere upstairs a door closes, gently, as if in answer.
